Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
MATHEMATICA LISTS:
Structured data objects\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["\<\

List groups a collection of 
expressions of any type.\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lexp\  = \ {a, b, c}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We have just defined a symbol lexp to be the list with elements a, \
b, c, which are themselves symbols. We can then do operations on them.\
\>", \
"Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lexp^2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sin[{a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[{a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Most inbuilt functions having the above property are called \
listable. By default, your own function will not be listable unless you \
change the attributes of your function.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Attributes[Sin]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(SetAttributes[f, Listable]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[{a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["List can be used as vectors:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\  + \ {1, 2, 3}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[lexp]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(First[lexp]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Last[lexp]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To refer to the ",
  StyleBox["i",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "-th element in a list, use the double square  bracket:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lexp[\([2]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the elements in a list are referred from 1, 2, ...."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Length[lexp]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lexp[\([0]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lexp[\([\(-1\)]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(rexp\  = \ {1, 2, 3}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Join[lexp, \ rexp]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Append[lexp, \ 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(AppendTo[lexp, 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Prepend[lexp, \ 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Lists are used to represent 
matrices as list of lists.\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(m = {{1, 2}, {0.5, 3}}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[m]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eigenvalues[m]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[m]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Transpose[m]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(Inverse[m]\)\(\ \)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(m\  . \ m\)], "Input"],

Cell["This is how a matrix multiplies a vector:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(m\  . \ {a, \ b}\)\(\ \)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["To access the elements an array, use [[i,j]]:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(m[\([1, 2]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(m[\([1]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell["List can be used to represent a tree.", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c\  = \ {\ {a, \ b}, \ {{Sin[x], \ 1}, \ 2}}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TreeForm[c]\)], "Input"],

Cell["List (or list of lists) v.s. associative array.", "Subtitle",
  TextAlignment->Left],

Cell["\<\
To use a list, the elements, size etc has to be defined first.  For \
example, the following use of two-dimensional array is not correct:\
\>", \
"Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[\([1, 2]\)]\  = \ 3\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Since h as a list of list does not exist yet.  We should say:\
\>", \
"Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((h\  = \ Table[0, {5}, {5}])\)\  // \ MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(\ \)\(h[\([1, 2]\)]\  = \ 3; \ MatrixForm[h]\)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Associative Array", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
For an associative array, you need not specify the size of array \
before hand. We use single square brackets for associative array.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a[0]\  = \ 2; \ a[\(-2\)]\  = \ \(-4\); \ a[1000]\  = \ w; \ 
    a[7, 7]\  = \ 7\)], "Input"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Expressions"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " expressions are internally always of the form"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(expr[expr1, \ expr2, \  ... ]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
where expr is called the Head, and expr1 is the first element of \
the expression, expr2 is the 2nd element. We obtain the expr by applying the \
Head[ ...] function, we obtain expr1 by First[...]. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[a + b]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(First[a + b]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FullForm[a + b]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plus[a, b]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FullForm[a*b\  + \ c \((1 + x)\)^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TreeForm[a*b\  + \ c \((1 + x)\)^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can think of practically any ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " expression as a tree. "
}], "Text"],

Cell["Assignment and Evaluation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a + b\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(b\  = \ \((1 + x)\)^2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a + b\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Clear[a, b]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a + b\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Remove[a, b]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This show you what variables (symbols) have been defined so far.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Global`*\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["PROGRAMMING IN MATHEMATICA",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Title"],

Cell["Programming Styles", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " supports a variety of programming styles. You can program in the spirit \
of C or Fortran; you may also do object oriented programming like C++; or you \
can do functional programming like LISP; and the rule-based programming style \
is powerful and can be concise. Here we demonstrate few ways to write a \
factorial function:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[m_Integer] := 
      Module[\ {i, \ n}, \ \n\t
        If[\ m\  < \ 0, \ \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tReturn[Infinity]]; \n\t
        n\  = \ 1; \n\t
        For[i\  = \ 1, \ i\  <= \ m, \ \(++i\), \ \[IndentingNewLine]\t
          n\  = \ n*i]; \[IndentingNewLine]\ \ \ \ \ n]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Some points: (1) use m only as a constant, (2) declare all \
variables except the argument as local, (3) return value is the last \
expression. Other issues relate to:
1. How do we want to display the output?
2. To what detail, do we need to check the validity of input?
We use the function as usual:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[6]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[\(-10\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Cheat code is to use built-in function:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac\  = \ Factorial\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ \(n!\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Or use rule-based programming:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ 
        Infinity\  /; \ n\  < \ 0;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(fac[0]\  = \ 1; fac[n_Integer]\  := n\ *\ fac[n - 1]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Or use functional programming style:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ Times @@ \ Range[n]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Procedural Programming Constructs", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Apart from syntactic differences, many of these constructs are similar to \
C.  The ",
  StyleBox["For",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " loop:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(For[initial, \ condition, \ 
      inc, \[IndentingNewLine]\ \ \ \ body]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_]\  := \ 
      Module[{t = 1, 
          i}, \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ For[i = 1, 
          i <= n, \(i++\), t *= i]; \ \[IndentingNewLine]t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(i\  = \ 3; \ While[\ i\  > 0, \ \(i--\); \ Print[i]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Which",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is like a sequence of if-else:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[x_]\  := \ 
      Which[x < \(-1\), \ \(-1\), \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tx <= 0, \ \(-1\)/
          2, \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tx > 0\  && \ x\  < 1, \ 
        1/2, \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tx\  >= \ 1, \ 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot[f[x], \ {x, \(-3\), 3}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["If",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " condition is in one of the three forms:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(If[test, \ then]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(If[test, \ then, \ else]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(If[test, \ then, \ else, \ unknown]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here test is an expression which evaluates to the symbol True or \
False; if test evaluates to True, the ``then'' expression is evaluated, \
otherwise, the ``else'' expression is evaluated.  If test is an expression \
other than True or False, unknown part will be evaluated. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(If[True, \ a, \ b, \ c]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Logical expressions can be formed similarly to C:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(\(<\)\(\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)\(less\ than\)\)\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(<=\)\(\ \)\(\(( \[LessEqual] )\)\ \ \ \ \ \ less\ equal\)\)\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(>\)\(\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)\(greater\ than\)\)\), \
"\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(>=\)\(\ \ \)\(\(( \[GreaterEqual] )\)\ \ \ \ \ greater\ \
equal\)\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(\[Equal]\)\(\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)\(equal\)\)\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(!=\)\(\ \)\(\(( \[NotEqual] \ )\)\ \ \ \ \ not\ equal\)\)\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\ \(\(&&\)\(\ \)\(\(( \[And] )\)\ \ \ \ \ AND\)\)\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\ \(\(||\)\(\ \)\(\(( \[Or] )\)\ \ \ \ \ OR\)\)\), \
"\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(===\)\(\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)\(identical\)\)\), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(\(=!=\)\(\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)\(not\ identical\)\)\)}], \
"Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x = 4; 
    If[x\  \[NotEqual] \ 5\  \[And] \ x\  > \ 2, \ Print[x]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The last two (===,=!=) is a stronger form of equal or not \
equal:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a \[Equal] b, \ a === b}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)^2\  === \ 1\  + \ 2  x\  + \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell["Typing of special symbols", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Special symbol like \[Pi] or \[Alpha] can be typed in \n\t(1) using \
palettes,\n\t(2) key strokes with ESC code ESC.  \n\te.g. \[Pi] is ESC p ESC, \
or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"T", 
          AdjustmentBox["E",
            BoxMargins->{{-0.075, -0.085}, {0, 0}},
            BoxBaselineShift->0.5], "X"}]]]],
  " style input ESC \\pi ESC.\nSuperscripts/subscripts can be typed in with \
Ctrl-^, Ctrl-_; Control-space get you back to normal type.  e.g.  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\_2. \)]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1\  + \ \[Alpha]\ )\)\^2 // \ Expand\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\[Pi]]\)], "Input"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"5.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 685}},
WindowSize->{1016, 651},
WindowMargins->{{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}},
ShowSelection->True,
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

